/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Isaiah chapters 51-55
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("isaiah", [
{n:51, name:"Look to the Rock", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 51:22",verse:"Thus saith thy Lord the LORD, and thy God that pleadeth the cause of his people, Behold, I have taken out of thine hand the cup of trembling, even the dregs of the cup of my fury; thou shalt no more drink it again:"}, facts:["Did you know that Abraham and Sarah are named here as the rock the exiles were hewn from, which means God's promise to one childless man is held up as proof He can revive a whole captive nation?","Did you know the Rahab cut by the arm of the LORD is not the woman of Jericho but an old poetic name for the chaos monster of the sea, picturing God's power over the waters at the Red Sea crossing?","Did you know the cup of fury is a common Bible picture for God's judgment, and here He promises to take it out of Jerusalem's own hand and put it into the hand of her oppressors?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is told to hearken at the start of Isaiah 51?", options:["Ye that follow after righteousness, ye that seek the LORD","The watchmen set upon the walls of Zion","The elders and princes of Judah","The sons of the prophets in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:1",verse:"Hearken to me, ye that follow after righteousness, ye that seek the LORD: look unto the rock whence ye are hewn, and to the hole of the pit whence ye are digged.", insight:"The call goes out to seekers, not to the already arrived. God speaks first to those who are reaching for righteousness, which means longing for Him is itself a sign of His drawing."},
{q:"What are the seekers told to look to?", options:["The rock whence ye are hewn, and the hole of the pit whence ye are digged","The mountains of Zion and the hills of Judah","The temple and the ark of the covenant","The river and the brooks of water"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:1",verse:"Hearken to me, ye that follow after righteousness, ye that seek the LORD: look unto the rock whence ye are hewn, and to the hole of the pit whence ye are digged.", insight:"Remembering where you were cut from keeps you humble and hopeful at once. God wants His people to look back at their lowly origin so they will trust His power to make something of them again."},
{q:"To whom should they look as the father and mother of the nation?", options:["Abraham your father, and unto Sarah that bare you","Isaac and Rebekah","Jacob and Rachel","Moses and Miriam"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:2",verse:"Look unto Abraham your father, and unto Sarah that bare you: for I called him alone, and blessed him, and increased him.", insight:"Abraham and Sarah are named together, the father of faith and the mother who laughed with joy. Looking to them means looking to a family story that began in impossibility and ended in a nation."},
{q:"How many did God call when He called Abraham?", options:["Abraham alone, whom He blessed and increased","Abraham with his whole household","Abraham and the seventy elders","Abraham with twelve princes"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:2",verse:"Look unto Abraham your father, and unto Sarah that bare you: for I called him alone, and blessed him, and increased him.", insight:"God started a nation from one man. When the work looks too big, this verse teaches that God has never needed large beginnings, only a willing one."},
{q:"What shall the LORD make Zion's wilderness like?", options:["Like Eden, and her desert like the garden of the LORD","Like the fields of Bethlehem at harvest","Like the cedars of Lebanon","Like a vineyard on a fruitful hill"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:3",verse:"For the LORD shall comfort Zion: he will comfort all her waste places; and he will make her wilderness like Eden, and her desert like the garden of the LORD; joy and gladness shall be found therein, thanksgiving, and the voice of melody.", insight:"God's comfort goes all the way back to Eden. He does not merely patch up ruins, He aims to restore paradise, which shows the scale of His saving purpose."},
{q:"What four things shall be found in comforted Zion?", options:["Joy, gladness, thanksgiving, and the voice of melody","Gold, silver, oil, and wine","Walls, towers, gates, and palaces","Peace, plenty, rest, and silence"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:3",verse:"For the LORD shall comfort Zion: he will comfort all her waste places; and he will make her wilderness like Eden, and her desert like the garden of the LORD; joy and gladness shall be found therein, thanksgiving, and the voice of melody.", insight:"Joy and melody are named as the marks of God's restoration, not just safety. Thanksgiving is the sound of a people who know they were rescued, and it belongs in the life of the comforted."},
{q:"Upon whom shall the isles wait?", options:["Upon me, saith the LORD","Upon the kings of the east","Upon the ships of Tarshish","Upon the elders of Zion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:5",verse:"My righteousness is near; my salvation is gone forth, and mine arms shall judge the people; the isles shall wait upon me, and on mine arm shall they trust.", insight:"The distant coastlands, far beyond Israel, are included in the waiting. God's salvation was never meant to stop at one nation's borders, and this verse quietly opens the door to the whole world."},
{q:"The heavens shall vanish away like what?", options:["Like smoke","Like a scroll rolled together","Like water poured on the ground","Like a dream in the night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:6",verse:"Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look upon the earth beneath: for the heavens shall vanish away like smoke, and the earth shall wax old like a garment, and they that dwell therein shall die in like manner: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not be abolished.", insight:"The sky itself is temporary beside God's salvation. Everything visible wears out, but what God promises to the righteous endures, a steady anchor when the world feels unstable."},
{q:"The earth shall wax old like what?", options:["Like a garment","Like a flower of the field","Like grass upon the housetops","Like wax before the fire"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:6",verse:"Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look upon the earth beneath: for the heavens shall vanish away like smoke, and the earth shall wax old like a garment, and they that dwell therein shall die in like manner: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not be abolished.", insight:"A garment grows thin and is folded away, and the earth will do the same. The contrast is deliberate: the world ages, but God's righteousness is never abolished."},
{q:"The people who know righteousness have what in their heart?", options:["My law","The fear of kings","The song of Moses","The tables of stone"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:7",verse:"Hearken unto me, ye that know righteousness, the people in whose heart is my law; fear ye not the reproach of men, neither be ye afraid of their revilings.", insight:"The law is not on their lips only but in their heart. Knowing righteousness means the word has moved inside, shaping what you love and not just what you recite."},
{q:"What should those who know righteousness not fear?", options:["The reproach of men","The famine and the sword","The beasts of the field","The darkness of the night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:7",verse:"Hearken unto me, ye that know righteousness, the people in whose heart is my law; fear ye not the reproach of men, neither be ye afraid of their revilings.", insight:"Mockery stings, but it cannot touch what God has settled. The verse frees the faithful from the tyranny of opinion, because the approval of men is too small a thing to rule a life."},
{q:"What shall eat the revilers up like a garment?", options:["The moth","The locust","The cankerworm","The palmerworm"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:8",verse:"For the moth shall eat them up like a garment, and the worm shall eat them like wool: but my righteousness shall be for ever, and my salvation from generation to generation.", insight:"The moth works slowly and unseen, yet it ruins the finest cloth. Those who mock God's people are not defeated by force here but by time and decay, while God's righteousness stands forever."},
{q:"Who is called to awake and put on strength?", options:["O arm of the LORD","O sword of the LORD","O voice of the LORD","O shield of the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:9",verse:"Awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD; awake, as in the ancient days, in the generations of old. Art thou not it that hath cut Rahab, and wounded the dragon?", insight:"The arm of the LORD is His power in action, and the prayer begs Him to act as He did in the ancient days. Faith can plead with God on the ground of what He has already done."},
{q:"Whom did the arm of the LORD cut, wounding the dragon?", options:["Rahab","Leviathan of the rivers","The king of Babylon","Pharaoh of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:9",verse:"Awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD; awake, as in the ancient days, in the generations of old. Art thou not it that hath cut Rahab, and wounded the dragon?", insight:"Rahab is the old chaos serpent, and God's victory over it stands for His mastery over every raging power. No enemy is too ancient or too deep for His arm to reach."},
{q:"What did the LORD dry to make a way for the ransomed?", options:["The sea, the waters of the great deep","The river Jordan","The brook Kidron","The pool of Siloam"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:10",verse:"Art thou not it which hath dried the sea, the waters of the great deep; that hath made the depths of the sea a way for the ransomed to pass over?", insight:"The Red Sea crossing is remembered as the pattern of all God's rescues. When you need a way where there is no way, this verse says the God who made a path through the sea is still the God you pray to."},
{q:"When the redeemed return to Zion with singing, what shall flee away?", options:["Sorrow and mourning","Hunger and thirst","War and tumult","Night and darkness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:11",verse:"Therefore the redeemed of the LORD shall return, and come with singing unto Zion; and everlasting joy shall be upon their head: they shall obtain gladness and joy; and sorrow and mourning shall flee away.", insight:"Sorrow does not linger in the presence of God's finished redemption, it flees. The verse pictures joy as something pursued and obtained, not merely waited for."},
{q:"Why should they not be afraid of man?", options:["Because man shall die, and the son of man shall be made as grass","Because man is far away in a distant land","Because man has no weapons of war","Because man will soon repent"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:12",verse:"I, even I, am he that comforteth you: who art thou, that thou shouldest be afraid of a man that shall die, and of the son of man which shall be made as grass;", insight:"Fear shrinks when you measure the threat honestly. A man who will die like grass cannot overrule the everlasting God, and remembering that proportion steadies the heart."},
{q:"What did the LORD put in the mouth of His servant?", options:["My words","A new song","The law of Moses","Bread from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:16",verse:"And I have put my words in thy mouth, and I have covered thee in the shadow of mine hand, that I may plant the heavens, and lay the foundations of the earth, and say unto Zion, Thou art my people.", insight:"God equips His servants by giving them His own words. The mission never depends on human cleverness, it depends on a message received from above and spoken in His name."},
{q:"What had Jerusalem drunk at the hand of the LORD?", options:["The cup of his fury, the dregs of the cup of trembling","The cup of blessing and thanksgiving","The wine of gladness","The water of life"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:17",verse:"Awake, awake, stand up, O Jerusalem, which hast drunk at the hand of the LORD the cup of his fury; thou hast drunken the dregs of the cup of trembling, and wrung them out.", insight:"Jerusalem drank judgment to the dregs, tasting it to the last drop. The image is sobering, sin has a bitter cup, but the chapter ends with that cup lifted away."},
{q:"What did Jerusalem's oppressors say to her soul?", options:["Bow down, that we may go over","Rise up, that we may serve thee","Flee away, that we may follow","Be silent, that we may rest"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 51:23",verse:"But I will put it into the hand of them that afflict thee; which have said to thy soul, Bow down, that we may go over: and thou hast laid thy body as the ground, and as the street, to them that went over.", insight:"The oppressors demanded that she become a pavement under their feet. God heard that demand, and His answer is to take the cup from her hand and place it in theirs, which is how perfectly His justice turns."}
]},
{n:52, name:"Thy God Reigneth", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 52:7",verse:"How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings, that publisheth peace; that bringeth good tidings of good, that publisheth salvation; that saith unto Zion, Thy God reigneth!"}, facts:["Did you know the beautiful feet of verse 7 are quoted by Paul in Romans 10:15, where he connects preaching the gospel with bringing good tidings to Zion?","Did you know the double cry, Depart ye, depart ye, echoes Israel's first exodus, except this time there is no hurried midnight flight, because the LORD Himself walks before and behind them?","Did you know the last three verses of this chapter begin the song of the suffering Servant, which continues through all of chapter 53?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is told to put on beautiful garments?", options:["O Jerusalem, the holy city","O Babylon, the golden city","O Tyre, the merchant city","O Samaria, the hill city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:1",verse:"Awake, awake; put on thy strength, O Zion; put on thy beautiful garments, O Jerusalem, the holy city: for henceforth there shall no more come into thee the uncircumcised and the unclean.", insight:"Jerusalem is called to dress for a new day, which means her shame is over. God does not leave His people in mourning clothes, He clothes them for the celebration of His coming."},
{q:"Who shall no more come into the holy city?", options:["The uncircumcised and the unclean","The merchants and the moneychangers","The strangers and the sojourners","The singers and the players"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:1",verse:"Awake, awake; put on thy strength, O Zion; put on thy beautiful garments, O Jerusalem, the holy city: for henceforth there shall no more come into thee the uncircumcised and the unclean.", insight:"Holiness means separation, and the restored city is guarded from defilement. God's people are called to keep that same guard over their own hearts, letting nothing unclean settle in."},
{q:"What is Zion told to shake herself from?", options:["The dust","The ashes","The sackcloth","The chains"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:2",verse:"Shake thyself from the dust; arise, and sit down, O Jerusalem: loose thyself from the bands of thy neck, O captive daughter of Zion.", insight:"Dust is where mourners and captives sit, so shaking it off is a decision to stop living as defeated. The verse teaches that restoration begins with rising, not with waiting to feel ready."},
{q:"What is the captive daughter of Zion told to loose from her neck?", options:["The bands of thy neck","The yoke of iron","The cord of affliction","The collar of brass"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:2",verse:"Shake thyself from the dust; arise, and sit down, O Jerusalem: loose thyself from the bands of thy neck, O captive daughter of Zion.", insight:"The bands are to be loosed by her own hand, she is not told to wait for rescue but to act on it. Freedom in God often requires our cooperation, stepping out of what He has already broken."},
{q:"Ye sold yourselves for nought; how shall ye be redeemed?", options:["Without money","With silver and gold","By the strength of kings","Through the labor of years"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:3",verse:"For thus saith the LORD, Ye have sold yourselves for nought; and ye shall be redeemed without money.", insight:"Redemption costs God everything and us nothing. We contributed nothing to our bondage's price and can contribute nothing to our release, which keeps salvation entirely a gift."},
{q:"Where did God's people go down aforetime to sojourn?", options:["Into Egypt","Into Assyria","Into Babylon","Into Moab"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:4",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD, My people went down aforetime into Egypt to sojourn there; and the Assyrian oppressed them without cause.", insight:"The pattern repeats: Egypt, then Assyria, then the present captivity. Remembering the old oppressions teaches that God has a long record of bringing His people out, and the current trouble is not the first."},
{q:"Who oppressed them without cause after Egypt?", options:["The Assyrian","The Philistine","The Moabite","The Edomite"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:4",verse:"For thus saith the Lord GOD, My people went down aforetime into Egypt to sojourn there; and the Assyrian oppressed them without cause.", insight:"The oppression was without cause, which means God's people suffered unjustly. When trouble comes without reason, this verse places it inside a story where God still sees and still acts."},
{q:"They that rule over them make them do what?", options:["Howl","Tremble","Flee","Mourn in silence"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:5",verse:"Now therefore, what have I here, saith the LORD, that my people is taken away for nought? they that rule over them make them to howl, saith the LORD; and my name continually every day is blasphemed.", insight:"God hears the howling of the oppressed, and their cry becomes His reason to act. The verse assures sufferers that their pain is not background noise to heaven, it is the summons of justice."},
{q:"In that day, what shall God's people know?", options:["My name","My judgments","My statutes","My wonders"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:6",verse:"Therefore my people shall know my name: therefore they shall know in that day that I am he that doth speak: behold, it is I.", insight:"Knowing God's name is knowing His character, His faithfulness made personal. The goal of redemption is not only freedom from bondage but intimacy with the Redeemer."},
{q:"What shall they know about the one who speaks to them?", options:["That I am he that doth speak: behold, it is I","That I am far off and hard to find","That I speak only through the prophets","That I am silent until the end"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:6",verse:"Therefore my people shall know my name: therefore they shall know in that day that I am he that doth speak: behold, it is I.", insight:"Behold, it is I is the most tender self-introduction in Scripture, God making sure His people recognize His voice. He wants to be known, not merely obeyed from a distance."},
{q:"What is beautiful upon the mountains?", options:["The feet of him that bringeth good tidings","The banners of the returning army","The towers of the holy city","The flocks on the green hills"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:7",verse:"How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him that bringeth good tidings, that publisheth peace; that bringeth good tidings of good, that publisheth salvation; that saith unto Zion, Thy God reigneth!", insight:"Even the feet of the messenger are beautiful, because the news they carry is salvation. How we deliver the gospel matters, our lives should make the message as lovely as the message itself."},
{q:"What shall the watchmen do when the LORD brings again Zion?", options:["Lift up the voice and sing together, for they shall see eye to eye","Sound the alarm and flee to the hills","Keep silent and watch from afar","Build the walls before the singing"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:8",verse:"Thy watchmen shall lift up the voice; with the voice together shall they sing: for they shall see eye to eye, when the LORD shall bring again Zion.", insight:"The watchmen see eye to eye, united in what they behold. True unity among God's people comes not from agreement on everything but from beholding the same returning Lord."},
{q:"Who shall break forth into joy and sing together?", options:["The waste places of Jerusalem","The merchants of the city","The kings of the earth","The armies of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:9",verse:"Break forth into joy, sing together, ye waste places of Jerusalem: for the LORD hath comforted his people, he hath redeemed Jerusalem.", insight:"Even the ruins sing, which means no place is too desolate for God's comfort to reach. Wherever your life lies in waste places, His redemption can make it resound with joy."},
{q:"In whose eyes has the LORD made bare His holy arm?", options:["In the eyes of all the nations","In the eyes of the priests alone","In the eyes of the elders of Judah","In the eyes of the prophets"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:10",verse:"The LORD hath made bare his holy arm in the eyes of all the nations; and all the ends of the earth shall see the salvation of our God.", insight:"God's salvation is staged before all nations, not hidden in a corner. He wants the whole earth to see His arm, which means our redemption is meant to be a witness, not a secret."},
{q:"What are the departing people told to touch not?", options:["No unclean thing","No gold of the temple","No bread of the stranger","No water of the well"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:11",verse:"Depart ye, depart ye, go ye out from thence, touch no unclean thing; go ye out of the midst of her; be ye clean, that bear the vessels of the LORD.", insight:"Leaving captivity means leaving its uncleanness behind too. You cannot carry Babylon's habits into Zion's freedom, and holiness requires leaving some things untouched."},
{q:"Who must be clean in the departure?", options:["They that bear the vessels of the LORD","They that carry the king's treasure","They that lead the flocks","They that keep the gates"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:11",verse:"Depart ye, depart ye, go ye out from thence, touch no unclean thing; go ye out of the midst of her; be ye clean, that bear the vessels of the LORD.", insight:"Those who handle holy things must be holy people. Service for God is never merely skill or office, it is character, and the vessels of the Lord deserve clean hands."},
{q:"Who goes before the people, and who guards behind them?", options:["The LORD will go before you, and the God of Israel will be your rereward","The angels will go before you, and the prophets guard behind","The captains will go before you, and the watchmen guard behind","The priests will go before you, and the Levites guard behind"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:12",verse:"For ye shall not go out with haste, nor go by flight: for the LORD will go before you; and the God of Israel will be your rereward.", insight:"God takes both the front and the rear, the lead and the guard. When He brings you out, you are covered on every side, which is why this exodus needs no haste and no fear."},
{q:"How shall God's servant deal, and how high shall he be?", options:["He shall deal prudently, and be exalted and extolled, and be very high","He shall deal harshly, and be feared in all lands","He shall deal secretly, and be hidden from kings","He shall deal swiftly, and be gone like the wind"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:13",verse:"Behold, my servant shall deal prudently, he shall be exalted and extolled, and be very high.", insight:"The servant's exaltation comes through prudence, wise and faithful dealing, not through force. God's way up is the way of faithful service, which is why the cross precedes the crown."},
{q:"How marred was the servant's visage and form?", options:["More marred than any man, and his form more than the sons of men","Like a leper before the priests","Like one wounded in battle","Like a stranger from a far country"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:14",verse:"As many were astonied at thee; his visage was so marred more than any man, and his form more than the sons of men:", insight:"His suffering was beyond all human measure, which tells us the price of our redemption was beyond all human paying. The depth of His wounding matches the depth of our need."},
{q:"What shall kings do before the servant?", options:["Shut their mouths at him","Bow their crowns at his feet","Offer him their treasures","Sing his praises aloud"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 52:15",verse:"So shall he sprinkle many nations; the kings shall shut their mouths at him: for that which had not been told them shall they see; and that which they had not heard shall they consider.", insight:"Kings fall silent because what they see silences all boasting. Human greatness has nothing to say in the presence of the servant's glory, a reminder that every mouth will one day be stopped before Him."}
]},
{n:53, name:"The Suffering Servant", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 53:5",verse:"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."}, facts:["Did you know the Ethiopian eunuch in Acts 8 was reading the words of verse 7 when Philip met him on the road, and asked the famous question, of whom speaketh the prophet this?","Did you know verse 9 says the servant made his grave with the wicked and with the rich, which Christians see fulfilled when Jesus was laid in the new tomb of Joseph of Arimathea, a rich man?","Did you know Handel set many lines of this chapter to music in Messiah, including All we like sheep and He was cut off out of the land of the living?"], questions:[
{q:"What two questions open Isaiah 53?", options:["Who hath believed our report? And to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?","Where is the holy mountain of the LORD? Who shall ascend into it?","What is man, that thou art mindful of him? Who shall stand in thy holy place?","When shall these things be? What shall be the sign of thy coming?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:1",verse:"Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of the LORD revealed?", insight:"The chapter begins with unbelief, not celebration. God's greatest act of salvation was hard to believe then and is hard to believe now, which is why faith itself is part of the gift."},
{q:"What two pictures describe how the servant grows up?", options:["As a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground","As a cedar in Lebanon, and as a palm by the waters","As a vine on a fruitful hill, and as an olive in the field","As a lily of the valley, and as a rose of Sharon"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:2",verse:"For he shall grow up before him as a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground: he hath no form nor comeliness; and when we shall see him, there is no beauty that we should desire him.", insight:"A root out of dry ground has nothing to feed on and nowhere to be admired. God's servant came without the advantages the world respects, teaching that true life does not depend on favorable conditions."},
{q:"What is said of the servant's appearance?", options:["He hath no form nor comeliness, and no beauty that we should desire him","He is fairer than the children of men","His countenance is as the sun in his strength","His eyes are as doves by the rivers"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:2",verse:"For he shall grow up before him as a tender plant, and as a root out of a dry ground: he hath no form nor comeliness; and when we shall see him, there is no beauty that we should desire him.", insight:"The world missed Him because He did not look like its idea of greatness. This warns us against judging by appearance, in people and in God's work, since glory often comes disguised."},
{q:"The servant is called a man of what?", options:["A man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief","A man of war, and acquainted with victory","A man of riches, and acquainted with pleasure","A man of wisdom, and acquainted with honor"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:3",verse:"He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not.", insight:"He did not merely visit sorrow, He was acquainted with it, like an old friend. Because He knows grief from the inside, no sufferer ever comes to Him as a stranger."},
{q:"What did we do with our faces when we saw him?", options:["We hid as it were our faces from him","We lifted up our faces to bless him","We set our faces to follow him","We bowed our faces to worship him"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:3",verse:"He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not.", insight:"Hiding the face is what people do at something too painful or shameful to look at. Our first response to the suffering servant was to look away, which is the honest confession this verse draws out of us."},
{q:"What did the servant bear, and what did he carry?", options:["Our griefs, and our sorrows","Our gold and our silver","Our lands and our houses","Our flocks and our herds"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:4",verse:"Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted.", insight:"He carried what was ours, not His own. Substitution is the heart of the verse: the griefs crushing us were lifted and laid on shoulders that had never earned them."},
{q:"How did we wrongly esteem him?", options:["Stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted","Blessed, honored of God, and exalted","Anointed, chosen of God, and crowned","Sent, approved of God, and received"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:4",verse:"Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted.", insight:"We mistook His suffering for His punishment, reading the cross exactly backward. The verse humbles our judgment, what looked like God's rejection was God's rescue."},
{q:"All we like what have gone astray?", options:["Like sheep","Like goats on the mountains","Like doves in the valleys","Like oxen in the stall"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:6",verse:"All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.", insight:"Sheep stray not by rebellion alone but by wandering, which is the more honest picture of most sin. The verse includes every one of us, all we, leaving no one outside the need for a shepherd."},
{q:"What did the LORD lay on him?", options:["The iniquity of us all","The riches of the nations","The crowns of the kings","The praises of the people"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:6",verse:"All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all.", insight:"The laying on was God's own act, the Father placing our sin on the Son. Salvation is not our climb to God but His deliberate transfer, our iniquity moved from us to Him."},
{q:"Like what was he brought to the slaughter?", options:["As a lamb","As a bullock for the altar","As a goat for the wilderness","As a dove for the offering"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:7",verse:"He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.", insight:"The lamb is the Passover animal, silent and without defense. Seeing Christ in this picture teaches that His death was not defeat but the appointed sacrifice, offered on purpose."},
{q:"How did he respond when oppressed and afflicted?", options:["He opened not his mouth, as a sheep before her shearers is dumb","He cried out for justice before the rulers","He called down fire from heaven","He pleaded his innocence day and night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:7",verse:"He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.", insight:"His silence was not weakness but submission, the quiet strength of one who trusts the Father's plan. When falsely accused, we are invited to learn His stillness instead of defending ourselves with noise."},
{q:"He was taken from prison and from what?", options:["From judgment","From the grave","From the temple","From the city"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:8",verse:"He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken.", insight:"Taken from prison and from judgment means His trial was a mockery of justice. The verse records that the innocent one was processed by guilty systems, which is why earthly verdicts are not the final word."},
{q:"For whose transgression was he stricken?", options:["For the transgression of my people","For the transgression of the priests","For the transgression of the kings","For the transgression of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:8",verse:"He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken.", insight:"My people, God's own, were the cause of the stroke, yet He was the one struck. The verse will not let us stand at a distance: His wounding was for us, and the confession must be personal."},
{q:"With whom did he make his grave?", options:["With the wicked, and with the rich in his death","With the kings, and with the princes in his death","With the prophets, and with the priests in his death","With the poor, and with the lowly in his death"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:9",verse:"And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth.", insight:"Buried with the wicked in intention but with the rich in fact, His grave tells two stories at once. Even in death God overruled men's plans, giving His servant an honored tomb."},
{q:"Why was such a burial unjust?", options:["Because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth","Because he had paid the temple tax in full","Because he had kept every feast of the law","Because he had honored father and mother"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:9",verse:"And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth.", insight:"No violence in His hands, no deceit on His lips: a perfectly clean life met a criminal's death. The injustice of it is the point, only a spotless one could be the offering for the spotted."},
{q:"Whom did it please to bruise him?", options:["The LORD","The rulers of the people","The soldiers of Rome","The chief priests"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:10",verse:"Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper in his hand.", insight:"It pleased the LORD, not men, to bruise Him, which means the cross was the Father's plan, not an accident. This is the hardest and sweetest truth here: our healing was purchased by God's deliberate will."},
{q:"When his soul is made an offering for sin, what is promised?", options:["He shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days","He shall see his grave, he shall sleep in peace","He shall see his enemies, he shall judge them","He shall see his riches, he shall enjoy them"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:10",verse:"Yet it pleased the LORD to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of the LORD shall prosper in his hand.", insight:"Death is not the end of His story: seed, prolonged days, and prospering pleasure follow the offering. The verse teaches that sacrifice in God's hands always leads to life beyond itself."},
{q:"What shall the righteous servant see of the travail of his soul?", options:["He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied","He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be forgotten","He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be ashamed","He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be weary"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:11",verse:"He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities.", insight:"He shall be satisfied, the suffering was worth it to Him. Knowing that Christ looks at the fruit of His pain with satisfaction gives our own trials a horizon of meaning."},
{q:"By his knowledge, what shall the righteous servant do for many?", options:["Justify many, for he shall bear their iniquities","Condemn many, for he shall judge their iniquities","Forget many, for he shall pass over their iniquities","Reward many, for he shall weigh their iniquities"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:11",verse:"He shall see of the travail of his soul, and shall be satisfied: by his knowledge shall my righteous servant justify many; for he shall bear their iniquities.", insight:"Justification comes by His knowledge, not by our performance. He bears the iniquities and we receive the righteousness, the great exchange on which every believer stands."},
{q:"With whom was he numbered, and what did he do for them?", options:["He was numbered with the transgressors, and made intercession for the transgressors","He was numbered with the righteous, and made peace for the righteous","He was numbered with the priests, and made atonement for the priests","He was numbered with the kings, and made covenant with the kings"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 53:12",verse:"Therefore will I divide him a portion with the great, and he shall divide the spoil with the strong; because he hath poured out his soul unto death: and he was numbered with the transgressors; and he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.", insight:"Crucified between criminals and praying for them, He intercedes for the very ones who put Him there. His last work on earth was prayer for His enemies, which sets the pattern for ours."}
]},
{n:54, name:"No Weapon Shall Prosper", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 54:17",verse:"No weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper; and every tongue that shall rise against thee in judgment thou shalt condemn. This is the heritage of the servants of the LORD, and their righteousness is of me, saith the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know God calls Himself Zion's husband in this chapter, a marriage picture of His covenant love that the New Testament later applies to Christ and the church?","Did you know verse 17's promise, that no weapon formed against thee shall prosper, has become one of the most quoted and prayed promises in the whole book of Isaiah?","Did you know the waters of Noah comparison means God's promise to Zion is as unbreakable as His oath after the flood that the waters would never again cover the earth?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is told to sing, though she did not bear?", options:["O barren, thou that didst not bear","O widow, thou that art alone","O maiden, thou that art young","O mother, thou that hast many"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:1",verse:"Sing, O barren, thou that didst not bear; break forth into singing, and cry aloud, thou that didst not travail with child: for more are the children of the desolate than the children of the married wife, saith the LORD.", insight:"The barren woman is told to sing before she sees the children, which means praise comes by promise, not by sight. Faith sings in the empty room because it trusts the One who promised to fill it."},
{q:"Why should the desolate break forth into singing?", options:["For more are the children of the desolate than the children of the married wife","For the winter is past and the spring is come","For the bridegroom rejoices over the bride","For the harvest of the field is great"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:1",verse:"Sing, O barren, thou that didst not bear; break forth into singing, and cry aloud, thou that didst not travail with child: for more are the children of the desolate than the children of the married wife, saith the LORD.", insight:"God's math runs backward from the world's: the desolate ends up with more children than the married wife. His grace specializes in filling what nature left empty."},
{q:"What is she told to do with the place of her tent?", options:["Enlarge it, stretch forth the curtains, lengthen the cords, and strengthen the stakes","Take it down, fold the curtains, shorten the cords, and loosen the stakes","Move it to the valley, pitch it low, and hide it from the wind","Leave it as it is, and wait for better days"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:2",verse:"Enlarge the place of thy tent, and let them stretch forth the curtains of thine habitations: spare not, lengthen thy cords, and strengthen thy stakes;", insight:"Enlarge before the growth arrives: preparation is an act of faith. Lengthening cords and strengthening stakes teaches that expecting God's blessing means making room for it now."},
{q:"On which sides shall she break forth?", options:["On the right hand and on the left","On the north side and on the south","Toward the east and toward the west","Upward to heaven and downward to the sea"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:3",verse:"For thou shalt break forth on the right hand and on the left; and thy seed shall inherit the Gentiles, and make the desolate cities to be inhabited.", insight:"Growth in every direction leaves no quarter of life untouched by God's blessing. The promise is not partial enlargement but a breaking forth that fills all sides."},
{q:"What two shames shall she forget and remember no more?", options:["The shame of thy youth, and the reproach of thy widowhood","The shame of thy fathers, and the reproach of thy neighbors","The shame of thy poverty, and the reproach of thy enemies","The shame of thy sickness, and the reproach of thy friends"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:4",verse:"Fear not; for thou shalt not be ashamed: neither be thou confounded; for thou shalt not be put to shame: for thou shalt forget the shame of thy youth, and shalt not remember the reproach of thy widowhood any more.", insight:"God promises not only to end the shame but to erase its memory. His forgiveness goes beyond pardon to healing, so the past no longer defines the future."},
{q:"Who is Zion's husband?", options:["Thy Maker, the LORD of hosts","Thy king, the son of David","Thy prophet, the servant of God","Thy priest, the son of Aaron"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:5",verse:"For thy Maker is thine husband; the LORD of hosts is his name; and thy Redeemer the Holy One of Israel; The God of the whole earth shall he be called.", insight:"Her husband is her Maker, which means the marriage cannot fail for lack of resources. The One who made her is committed to her, and a Creator's love does not run out."},
{q:"What three names is her Redeemer given?", options:["The Holy One of Israel, the God of the whole earth","The King of kings, the Lord of lords","The Ancient of days, the Father of lights","The Prince of peace, the Everlasting Father"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:5",verse:"For thy Maker is thine husband; the LORD of hosts is his name; and thy Redeemer the Holy One of Israel; The God of the whole earth shall he be called.", insight:"He is the Holy One of Israel and also the God of the whole earth, near and universal at once. The verse widens our vision: the Redeemer of one people is the God of all the world."},
{q:"How did the LORD call her?", options:["As a woman forsaken and grieved in spirit, a wife of youth when refused","As a queen crowned and honored in the palace","As a servant hired for wages in the field","As a child taught and trained in the house"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:6",verse:"For the LORD hath called thee as a woman forsaken and grieved in spirit, and a wife of youth, when thou wast refused, saith thy God.", insight:"God meets her in the exact shape of her grief, forsaken and refused. He does not wait for her to dress up the pain; He calls her by the true name of her sorrow and answers it."},
{q:"For how long did the LORD forsake her?", options:["For a small moment","For forty years","For seventy years","For a whole generation"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:7",verse:"For a small moment have I forsaken thee; but with great mercies will I gather thee.", insight:"A small moment against great mercies: God's anger is brief, His kindness everlasting. When discipline comes, this proportion teaches us to endure the moment with the mercy in view."},
{q:"What stands against the little wrath in verse 8?", options:["Everlasting kindness","A second chastening","A longer exile","A heavier yoke"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:8",verse:"In a little wrath I hid my face from thee for a moment; but with everlasting kindness will I have mercy on thee, saith the LORD thy Redeemer.", insight:"Little wrath, everlasting kindness: the contrast is the whole gospel of God's heart. His correction is momentary, but His mercy has no end, and that is what we are to build our hope on."},
{q:"To what does God compare His promise to Zion?", options:["The waters of Noah","The covenant with Abraham","The oath to David","The sign of Jonah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:9",verse:"For this is as the waters of Noah unto me: for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah should no more go over the earth; so have I sworn that I would not be wroth with thee, nor rebuke thee.", insight:"The flood oath is the strongest promise God ever swore to the earth, and He sets His promise to Zion beside it. If you doubt His word to you, look at the rainbow and remember how He keeps oaths."},
{q:"What did God swear concerning the waters of Noah?", options:["They should no more go over the earth","They should return every spring","They should water the deserts","They should cleanse the nations"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:9",verse:"For this is as the waters of Noah unto me: for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah should no more go over the earth; so have I sworn that I would not be wroth with thee, nor rebuke thee.", insight:"No more: the flood will never be repeated, and neither will His wrath against Zion. God's oaths are the ground of our security, because He cannot swear and then unsay it."},
{q:"What shall depart and be removed, even if God's kindness does not?", options:["The mountains shall depart, and the hills be removed","The sun shall depart, and the moon be removed","The rivers shall depart, and the seas be removed","The stars shall depart, and the clouds be removed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:10",verse:"For the mountains shall depart, and the hills be removed; but my kindness shall not depart from thee, neither shall the covenant of my peace be removed, saith the LORD that hath mercy on thee.", insight:"Mountains are the Bible's picture of permanence, yet they will move before His kindness does. When everything solid in life shifts, this verse names the one thing that cannot."},
{q:"What two things shall not depart from Zion?", options:["My kindness, and the covenant of my peace","My judgments, and the rod of my anger","My statutes, and the charge of my law","My wonders, and the signs of my power"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:10",verse:"For the mountains shall depart, and the hills be removed; but my kindness shall not depart from thee, neither shall the covenant of my peace be removed, saith the LORD that hath mercy on thee.", insight:"Kindness and peace are covenanted, bound by promise, not by our performance. The security of the believer rests here: God's commitment to us is contractual in His faithfulness."},
{q:"With what will God lay Zion's foundations?", options:["With sapphires, and stones of fair colors","With gold, and silver from Tarshish","With cedar, and beams of cypress","With marble, and pillars of ivory"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:11",verse:"O thou afflicted, tossed with tempest, and not comforted, behold, I will lay thy stones with fair colors, and lay thy foundations with sapphires.", insight:"The afflicted, tempest-tossed city gets foundations of sapphire, beauty built into its very base. God does not merely repair the broken, He rebuilds with splendor."},
{q:"What shall her windows, gates, and borders be made of?", options:["Windows of agates, gates of carbuncles, and borders of pleasant stones","Windows of crystal, gates of brass, and borders of iron","Windows of gold, gates of silver, and borders of pearl","Windows of glass, gates of wood, and borders of clay"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:12",verse:"And I will make thy windows of agates, and thy gates of carbuncles, and all thy borders of pleasant stones.", insight:"Every part of the city, windows, gates, borders, is made precious. God's restoration is thorough, touching the places of seeing, entering, and dwelling, not just the facade."},
{q:"What is promised concerning Zion's children?", options:["All thy children shall be taught of the LORD, and great shall be the peace of thy children","All thy children shall be mighty in war, and great shall be their victories","All thy children shall be rich in goods, and great shall be their storehouses","All thy children shall be rulers of nations, and great shall be their dominion"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:13",verse:"And all thy children shall be taught of the LORD; and great shall be the peace of thy children.", insight:"Taught of the LORD is the greatest inheritance a parent can want for a child. Peace follows teaching, because hearts instructed by God are hearts at rest."},
{q:"From what two things shall she be far?", options:["From oppression, and from terror","From hunger, and from thirst","From labor, and from sorrow","From winter, and from night"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:14",verse:"In righteousness shalt thou be established: thou shalt be far from oppression; for thou shalt not fear: and from terror; for it shall not come near thee.", insight:"Established in righteousness, she is set far from oppression and terror. Righteousness is not only right standing but safety, a foundation that fear cannot approach."},
{q:"What shall happen to whoever gathers together against her?", options:["They shall fall for thy sake","They shall be turned back in shame","They shall be scattered to the winds","They shall be bound with chains"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:15",verse:"Behold, they shall surely gather together, but not by me: whosoever shall gather together against thee shall fall for thy sake.", insight:"Gatherings against God's people fall, not by our might but for thy sake, because He has taken our cause as His own. Opposition to the saints is opposition to the Lord who guards them."},
{q:"Whom has God created, showing even enemies' tools are in His hand?", options:["The smith that bloweth the coals, and the waster to destroy","The archer that bendeth the bow, and the slinger of stones","The horseman that rideth to war, and the chariot of iron","The spy that creepeth in darkness, and the traitor at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 54:16",verse:"Behold, I have created the smith that bloweth the coals in the fire, and that bringeth forth an instrument for his work; and I have created the waster to destroy.", insight:"Even the smith who forges weapons and the waster who destroys are God's creatures. No enemy operates outside His sovereignty, which means no threat is ever truly out of control."}
]},
{n:55, name:"Come to the Waters", seal:{ref:"Isaiah 55:11",verse:"So shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it."}, facts:["Did you know the free invitation of verse 1, to buy without money and without price, is the gospel picture of salvation as a pure gift, echoed in Revelation's call to let him that is athirst come?","Did you know Paul quoted the sure mercies of David in Acts 13:34, applying this promise to the resurrection of Jesus?","Did you know verse 11's promise that God's word shall not return unto Him void is prayed by preachers everywhere before they open their Bibles to teach?"], questions:[
{q:"Who is invited to come to the waters?", options:["Every one that thirsteth, and he that hath no money","Every one that hungers, and he that hath no bread","Every one that labors, and he that hath no rest","Every one that mourns, and he that hath no comfort"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:1",verse:"Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters, and he that hath no money; come ye, buy, and eat; yea, come, buy wine and milk without money and without price.", insight:"The invitation goes to the thirsty and the broke, the two qualifications being need and emptiness. God's feast is not for the full but for the thirsty, which means your need is your invitation."},
{q:"What may they buy without money and without price?", options:["Wine and milk","Bread and honey","Oil and wine","Corn and oil"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:1",verse:"Ho, every one that thirsteth, come ye to the waters, and he that hath no money; come ye, buy, and eat; yea, come, buy wine and milk without money and without price.", insight:"Wine for joy and milk for nourishment, both free. The best things in God's kingdom cannot be purchased, which keeps the proud out and lets the poorest in."},
{q:"What searching question opens verse 2?", options:["Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread?","Wherefore do ye fear the night and its terrors?","Wherefore do ye run to the hills for safety?","Wherefore do ye weep for the years that are gone?"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:2",verse:"Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread? and your labour for that which satisfieth not? hearken diligently unto me, and eat ye that which is good, and let your soul delight itself in fatness.", insight:"We spend our wages on what cannot feed us, chasing satisfactions that never satisfy. The question exposes our misspent lives so we will turn our hunger toward what truly fills."},
{q:"In what shall your soul delight itself?", options:["In fatness","In sweetness","In softness","In stillness"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:2",verse:"Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread? and your labour for that which satisfieth not? hearken diligently unto me, and eat ye that which is good, and let your soul delight itself in fatness.", insight:"Fatness is the Bible's word for rich abundance, the opposite of spiritual starvation. God does not offer bare survival but delight, a soul well fed and deeply glad."},
{q:"What will happen if they incline their ear and hear?", options:["Your soul shall live","Your house shall stand","Your name shall endure","Your years shall be many"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:3",verse:"Incline your ear, and come unto me: hear, and your soul shall live; and I will make an everlasting covenant with you, even the sure mercies of David.", insight:"Hearing gives life: the ear is the doorway to the soul. This is why preaching matters so much, because God has joined life itself to the hearing of His word."},
{q:"What covenant will God make with them?", options:["An everlasting covenant, even the sure mercies of David","A covenant of salt, even the statutes of Moses","A covenant of peace, even the charge of Aaron","A covenant of grace, even the promise to Noah"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:3",verse:"Incline your ear, and come unto me: hear, and your soul shall live; and I will make an everlasting covenant with you, even the sure mercies of David.", insight:"The sure mercies of David are the unfailing promises made to David's house, now offered to all who come. God's covenant faithfulness to one king becomes the ground of mercy for every thirsty soul."},
{q:"What three offices was he given to the people?", options:["A witness to the people, a leader and commander to the people","A priest to the people, a teacher and judge of the people","A prophet to the people, a shepherd and keeper of the people","A king to the people, a warrior and defender of the people"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:4",verse:"Behold, I have given him for a witness to the people, a leader and commander to the people.", insight:"Witness, leader, commander: David's greater Son testifies the truth, leads the way, and commands the battle. Christ fills all three, which is why following Him is both safe and sure."},
{q:"Whom shall she call?", options:["A nation that thou knowest not","A brother that thou hast lost","A neighbor that thou hast wronged","A servant that thou hast freed"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:5",verse:"Behold, thou shalt call a nation that thou knowest not, and nations that knew not thee shall run unto thee because of the LORD thy God, and for the Holy One of Israel; for he hath glorified thee.", insight:"She will call a nation she never knew, which means God's people are always expanding beyond their borders. The church's mission was written into the prophets long before it sailed in ships."},
{q:"Why shall unknown nations run unto her?", options:["Because of the LORD thy God, and for the Holy One of Israel, for he hath glorified thee","Because of her riches and her strong walls","Because of her armies and her chariots","Because of her wisdom and her learning"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:5",verse:"Behold, thou shalt call a nation that thou knowest not, and nations that knew not thee shall run unto thee because of the LORD thy God, and for the Holy One of Israel; for he hath glorified thee.", insight:"Nations run not to her strength but to her God, drawn by His glory on her. When God glorifies His people, the world takes notice, which makes holiness the church's true attraction."},
{q:"When should they seek the LORD?", options:["While he may be found","While the sun is shining","While the temple stands","While the prophets speak"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:6",verse:"Seek ye the LORD while he may be found, call ye upon him while he is near:", insight:"While he may be found carries a holy urgency, the day of seeking will not last forever. Today is the appointed time, and delay is the quietest way to refuse."},
{q:"When should they call upon Him?", options:["While he is near","While the priests are praying","While the sacrifices burn","While the feasts are kept"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:6",verse:"Seek ye the LORD while he may be found, call ye upon him while he is near:", insight:"He is near now, not distant and not delayed. The verse corrects the feeling that God is far off: nearness is the present fact, and calling is the present duty."},
{q:"What must the wicked forsake?", options:["His way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts","His riches, and the covetous man his gold","His house, and the wanderer his home","His friends, and the lonely man his company"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:7",verse:"Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts: and let him return unto the LORD, and he will have mercy upon him; and to our God, for he will abundantly pardon.", insight:"Forsaking must reach the thoughts, not just the deeds, because sin lives in the mind before it walks in the way. True repentance goes deeper than behavior, it surrenders the inner life."},
{q:"What will God do for the one who returns?", options:["He will have mercy upon him, and will abundantly pardon","He will test him for a season, then receive him","He will remember his sins for a little while","He will accept him but keep him at a distance"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:7",verse:"Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts: and let him return unto the LORD, and he will have mercy upon him; and to our God, for he will abundantly pardon.", insight:"Abundantly pardon: the pardon overflows the sin. No one returns to find a reluctant God, He pardons like He gives, in abundance beyond what we dared to ask."},
{q:"What does the LORD say about His thoughts and ours?", options:["My thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways","My thoughts are above your thoughts, yet your ways please me","My thoughts are like your thoughts, only greater","My thoughts are hidden, and your ways are known"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:8",verse:"For my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways, saith the LORD.", insight:"The gap between His thoughts and ours is not a rebuke but an invitation to trust. When His ways confuse us, this verse reminds us that confusion is expected, and faith walks on anyway."},
{q:"How much higher are God's ways than ours?", options:["As the heavens are higher than the earth","As the mountains are higher than the valleys","As the eagle flies higher than the sparrow","As the sea is deeper than the river"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:9",verse:"For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are my ways higher than your ways, and my thoughts than your thoughts.", insight:"The heavens-to-earth measure makes the distance infinite, not merely large. This is why prayer ends with trust rather than full understanding, His wisdom outruns ours forever."},
{q:"What comes down from heaven in verse 10?", options:["The rain, and the snow","The hail, and the frost","The dew, and the mist","The thunder, and the lightning"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:10",verse:"For as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven, and returneth not thither, but watereth the earth, and maketh it bring forth and bud, that it may give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater:", insight:"Rain and snow fall on their own schedule, accomplishing their work whether watched or not. God's word works the same way, quietly and certainly, beyond our management."},
{q:"What does the watered earth give?", options:["Seed to the sower, and bread to the eater","Wine to the drinker, and oil to the anointed","Grass to the cattle, and herbs to the gatherer","Flowers to the garden, and shade to the traveler"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:10",verse:"For as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven, and returneth not thither, but watereth the earth, and maketh it bring forth and bud, that it may give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater:", insight:"Seed for future sowing and bread for present eating: God's provision covers both tomorrow and today. His word does the same, feeding today's need while planting tomorrow's harvest."},
{q:"How shall they go out, and how shall they be led forth?", options:["With joy, and be led forth with peace","With songs, and be led forth with dancing","With riches, and be led forth with honor","With strength, and be led forth with courage"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:12",verse:"For ye shall go out with joy, and be led forth with peace: the mountains and the hills shall break forth before you into singing, and all the trees of the field shall clap their hands.", insight:"Joy for the going and peace for the journey: God supplies both the start and the road. The Christian life is not a grim march but a joyful going out under peaceful leading."},
{q:"What shall the trees of the field do?", options:["Clap their hands","Bow their heads","Wave their branches","Sing their songs"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:12",verse:"For ye shall go out with joy, and be led forth with peace: the mountains and the hills shall break forth before you into singing, and all the trees of the field shall clap their hands.", insight:"The trees clap their hands because all creation joins the redemption song. When God's people go out with joy, even nature applauds, a picture of the whole world set right."},
{q:"What shall come up instead of the thorn and the brier?", options:["The fir tree instead of the thorn, and the myrtle tree instead of the brier","The cedar instead of the thorn, and the olive instead of the brier","The palm instead of the thorn, and the fig instead of the brier","The oak instead of the thorn, and the vine instead of the brier"], a:0, ref:"Isaiah 55:13",verse:"Instead of the thorn shall come up the fir tree, and instead of the brier shall come up the myrtle tree: and it shall be to the LORD for a name, for an everlasting sign that shall not be cut off.", insight:"Thorns are the curse of Genesis, and here they are replaced by beautiful trees. God's final word over the curse is reversal, what sin made painful He makes lovely."}
]}
]);
